WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:05.500
so yesterday, CNN held back the back climate
change town halls with the number of the 2020

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:06.500
democratic [inaudible] the

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:10.300
candidates and it just went on for hours and
hours and I sort of dipped in and out of it

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:13.100
during a twitch live stream that I did last
night.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:16.910
It was relatively uneventful, but there were
some interesting things that happened.

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:21.920
Um, the first one I want to mention a one
notable trend is that it again wasn't great

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:26.880
for Joe Biden and I don't want to make it
seem like I'm piling on Biden cause there

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.650
were also other candidates who have no shot
who didn't do well.

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:32.920
Like Amy Klobuchar I don't think did very
well during this thing.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:36.260
Carla I don't think did well although she
is in fourth place and I guess you can say

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:38.210
see she, she still has a shot.

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:42.760
Uh, the, the point is Biden is leading in
the polls and he really didn't have a good

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:44.160
climate change town hall.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:48.780
This was seven hours of back-to-back-to-back
town halls with candidates.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:54.820
The idea was a good one, which is let's go
in depth on the issue of climate with each

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:55.880
candidate.

00:00:55.880 --> 00:01:00.910
This is in a really important issue and we
want to know who can go deep on these topics.

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:01.910
Great.

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:03.530
Carla Harris had some weird moments.

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:07.880
She had one about plastic and paper straws
that was sort of odd.

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:13.130
Andrew Yang was asked about quantifying carbon
dioxide reduction that we can get from Geo

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:17.130
Engineering, which, uh, he has pushed and
talked about.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:18.470
He wasn't able to answer that.

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:19.470
Okay?

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:20.470
Some people said it was a stumble.

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:21.470
You should've been prepared.

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:22.659
Biden really didn't do well.

00:01:22.659 --> 00:01:24.930
He was, again, not answering questions clearly.

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:31.079
And there was this moment when what was clearly
a planned attack question was put to Joe Biden.

00:01:31.079 --> 00:01:32.079
Take a look.

00:01:32.079 --> 00:01:36.900
I have to ask, how can we trust you to hold
these corporations and executives accountable

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:42.119
for their crimes against humanity when we
know that tomorrow you are holding high dollar

00:01:42.119 --> 00:01:45.799
fundraiser hosted by Andrew Goldman, a fossil
fuel executive [inaudible]

00:01:45.799 --> 00:01:52.470
he is not a fossil philosophy.

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:56.509
And the fact of the matter is that, uh, what
we talk about is what are we going to do about

00:01:56.509 --> 00:01:57.509
those corporations?

00:01:57.509 --> 00:01:58.899
What have we done?

00:01:58.899 --> 00:02:02.090
And [inaudible] everywhere along the way.

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:07.049
For example, I've argued and we've, and pushed
for us suing those executives who are engaged

00:02:07.049 --> 00:02:10.110
in pollution, those companies who want to
engage in pollution.

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:11.110
So

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:12.110
Biden says, wait, wait, wait.

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:18.000
Andrew Goldman isn't a fossil fuel executive
and the relevance to this entire conversation

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:23.910
is that Joe Biden pledge not to take campaign
funds from fossil fuel executives and if indeed

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:29.530
he is, uh, involved with Andrew Goldman fossil
fuel executive and money is being raised and

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:32.950
money is being donated, that would appear
to be a violation of Joe Biden's pledge.

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:38.879
Now it was later sort of clarified weirdly
by Anderson Cooper, not by Joe Biden that

00:02:38.879 --> 00:02:44.860
Andrew Goldman is not a fossil fuel executive,
but he is a fossil fuel investor.

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:51.050
So technically Joe Biden's pledge remains
in force I guess because he is not taking

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:54.870
the money from executives but rather from
investors.

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:59.110
It's one of these situations though where
you really need to understand the dynamics

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:00.270
of what took place.

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:04.841
First of all, this was by every indication
one of these planned attacks on Biden, but

00:03:04.841 --> 00:03:07.700
it was effective and the clarification came
later.

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:12.340
The clarification came from Anderson Cooper
rather than from Biden himself.

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:18.280
Substantively, I don't think that anything
shocking was revealed at this town hall about

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:24.050
Biden or anyone else, but the longer format
for each candidate does let us get more of

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:30.080
a sense of how deep their knowledges, the
knowledge in climate from lots of the candidates

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:31.769
is quite frankly not very deep.

00:03:31.769 --> 00:03:35.239
And I think that that should alarm all of
us given that it is such a major humanitarian

00:03:35.239 --> 00:03:40.060
crisis and an issue of national security and
an issue of geopolitics.

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:41.210
And it's disappointing.

00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:43.299
Now I'm not saying Trump has any better idea.

00:03:43.299 --> 00:03:46.610
Trump writes the entire thing off as a hoax
created by the Chinese.

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:47.980
That's a disaster as well.

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:52.709
At least the people on yesterday's stages
acknowledge the problem that there is.

00:03:52.709 --> 00:03:55.549
Uh, but uh, this was not a good moment for
Joe Biden.

00:03:55.549 --> 00:03:57.659
Then just bad luck and timing by Biden.

00:03:57.659 --> 00:03:59.459
This act, I actually feel bad about this.

00:03:59.459 --> 00:04:04.580
He burst a blood vessel in his eye during
the event which was noticeable on television

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:10.459
that led to the obviously predictable slew
of allegations that Joe Biden is sick, sort

00:04:10.459 --> 00:04:13.090
of like the Hillary Parkinson's type stuff.

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:15.010
That's all completely to be expected.

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:19.590
And there are sort of two perspectives on
this anti Biden stuff that are growing on

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:20.640
the left.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:26.050
There are those who are against Biden because
he's simply a centrist.

00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:29.200
People are more progressive and they say,
I want someone more progressive in Biden's

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:31.990
sort of a corporate center, left Democrat.

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:35.610
There are others who oppose Joe Biden because
they believe that Joe Biden is not electable.

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:38.570
In other words, that he could not beat Donald
Trump in the general election.

00:04:38.570 --> 00:04:43.270
Now, the politics part, I understand Biden
is not nearly the most progressive candidate.

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:46.910
In fact, he's one of the most centrist candidates.

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:49.650
That is simply a reality.

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:55.040
The Biden can't be Trump stuff requires a
little bit of fortune telling and seeing into

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:56.260
the future.

00:04:56.260 --> 00:04:59.639
But if you look at the hypothetical polling
match-ups right now against Trump, Biden is

00:04:59.639 --> 00:05:01.590
routinely doing the best period.

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:05.460
And the reason why is not as progressivism
clearly or is lack thereof.

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:07.330
It's a demographic issue more than anything
else.

00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:12.400
Older people vote much more than younger people,
and they vote much more reliably than younger

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:13.470
people.

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:19.020
And when you look at the demographics of who
is supporting Joe Biden, uh, you understand

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:23.540
that any likely voter model that accounts
for how likely people are to vote, uh, does

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:24.540
help.

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:28.000
Joe Biden and Joe Biden in the hypothetical
match-ups does the best against Donald Trump

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:29.000
than anybody else.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:30.580
Now, Bernie is not far behind.

00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:33.280
Warren is not far behind, uh, Bernie.

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:36.180
But it's important to understand where those
numbers come from.

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:40.320
And I know that every time we talk about this,
I get emails from people saying, David, you

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:45.150
are drastically underestimating that young
people this time around are going to be the

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:46.169
difference maker.

00:05:46.169 --> 00:05:47.340
I hope that's the case.

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:49.090
I hope that that's the case.

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:55.040
But every election cycle we hear that and
rarely does it actually a have become a reality.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:58.720
Older people are the reliable voters and the
younger people simply are not.

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:03.050
I hope it is different, but understand why
a likely voter model would not assume that

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:07.350
because we talk about it every election cycle
and it basically doesn't end up happening.

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:11.169
Now let's talk more broadly about one of the
macro issues.

00:06:11.169 --> 00:06:12.410
That is a huge red herring.

00:06:12.410 --> 00:06:13.350
In the discussions of climate.

